HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:35912:   "note": "DEFECT, HIGH.  cpu_m88k.c, anchor `vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented': only RESET/INTERRUPT/INSTRUCTION_ACCESS/DATA_ACCESS set `implemented = true'; nine other switch cases print a VERBOSITY_ERROR and break, so the fallback sets running=false on every CPU and returns (emul.c then breaks the main loop: a GRACEFUL guest-drivable machine halt, not an abort).  Pre-existing upstream (`git log -L' on the fallback returns only the 0.7.0 import); exposed by the #380 review, NOT introduced by #323/#380.  Reachable classes by raise site (deriver: grep -o 'm88k_exception(cpu, *M88K_EXCEPTION_[A-Z0-9_]+' src/cpus/cpu_m88k*.c | sort | uniq -c): PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION 10 (ldcr/stcr/xcr/rte/ld.usr/st.usr/tb0/tb1/tcnd in user mode), ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE 4 (divu/div by zero, any mode), INTEGER_OVERFLOW 3 (add/sub, any mode), MISALIGNED_ACCESS 1, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE 1 (prom_call in user mode); BOUNDS_CHECK and ERROR have no LITERAL-CONSTANT raise site -- but a supervisor tb0/tb1/tcnd names any 9-bit vector directly (`m88k_exception(cpu, ic->arg[2], 1)'), so both ARE reachable today (MEASURED on pass 1: supervisor `tb1 0,r3,7' halts HEAD; an earlier wording here said 'NO raise site today (latent)' -- corrected by #502's pass 1).  ERROR is additionally reached by the SFRZ re-route when a handler faults.  Vector offsets verified against Table 6-1 (mc88100.txt:8524-8538): PRIVILEGE 0x30, ILLEGAL_DIVIDE 0x40, OVERFLOW 0x48, MISALIGNED 0x20.  DISPOSITION: DELIVER (not a device-style decline -- the guest OS has handlers for these): `implemented = true' for the five, flowing through the existing common path (SFRZ, EPSR, SXIP/SNIP/SFIP shadow, supervisor switch, pc = VBR + 8*vector); MISALIGNED already fills DMT in the load/store template.  WITNESS OWED AT RUNG 3 BEFORE ANY EDIT: cold-debugger probe on committed testm88k or luna-88k (exemplars regress/m88k_idle_probe.py, m88k_rounding_probe.py): supervisor `divu r2,r3,r0', VBR into RAM, breakpoint at VBR+0x40, free-run -- pre-fix `unimplemented vector=0x008' and the machine stops; post-fix PC == VBR+0x40, SXIP = faulting instruction | XIP_V, PSR shadow frozen, machine running; plus a LIVENESS control (an already-delivered vector) and an ATTRIBUTION control (the same instruction on a non-faulting operand does not vector).  Split from m88kexcsfu1 (one fix design per round).  Ranked at the top of the live-defect tier, just below the exit(1)-abort class (this one stops gracefully).  Provenance: retrospective re-check (owner directive 2026-09-04) batch 2 -- GPT-6 Astra (codex, max effort) read-only review, adjudicated by Fable 5.1; artifacts regress/artifacts/retro_20260905/batch2_m88k/ (astra_batch2.txt, fable_adj_batch2.md).  The other seven panel seats were DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the retrospective (owner scope); the fix round runs the full nine-seat panel.  ROUND #502 (2026-09-05): SEVEN classes delivered (the five plus BOUNDS and ERROR), at BOTH sites (switch-1 `implemented' AND a tail `case' -- the one-site form was MEASURED to stop the cpu at the tail default).  Detector regress/m88k_exc_probe.py v3 (22 fault rows + 2 controls; rows resid50/sfu1h EXPECT HALT as negative controls) wired into gate 11; HEAD 0/9 -> 0/10 -> 3/22 (the two HALT controls + the pre-existing user vector 128), fix 9/9 -> 10/10 -> 22/22.  Three kill tables + the Opus pass-2 table under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/: table 1 exposed M_ERRORDROP (repaired: errortb), the Opus pass-2 table exposed M_NOVBR / M_SFRZTRAP / M_SFIPNOTGT / M_DSLOTSWAP (all 10/10; repaired: vbrtb, trapfrozen, dslot br.n +16), pass-2 seats exposed the trap-route blindness (repaired: misaligntb/unimpltb/div0tb/ovftb/privsuptb), the carry half of F14 (ovfc), the LEVEL channel (Fable: INFO vs ERROR IS observable at default verbosity), the `<=' tail-guard bound (usertb) and the missing negative controls (kimi: resid50, sfu1h); table 3 killed every one of those mutants; M_TRAPONLY EQUIVALENT (disclosed, m88kexcdebt).  Filed from the round: m88kdslotshadow MEDIUM, m88kexcfrozendmt LOW, m88kexchwtraps LOW, m88kexcdebt LOW.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: `commits` WAS EMPTY ON A **HIGH** ROW WHOSE FIX IS COMMITTED.  Set to `79cd12e`. ***\n\n**This row's own title says one guest `divu rX,rY,r0` from USER MODE ends the emulation** -- and the record said the work had not shipped.  *A reader trusting `commits` reads a live user-reachable emulator-halt as outstanding.*\n\n=== VERIFIED THREE INDEPENDENT WAYS BEFORE WRITING, because a correlate is not the thing ===\n  1. **The tag is in committed source, and it is a FIX rather than a reference.**  `cpu_m88k.c` carries **seven** `implemented = true;  /*  m88kexcdeliver  */` lines -- at MISALIGNED_ACCESS, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE, PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION, BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE, INTEGER_OVERFLOW and ERROR -- each an ASSIGNMENT that changes behaviour, not prose in a comment.\n  2. **Line history settles the commit**: `git log -L 728,731:src/cpus/cpu_m88k.c` gives exactly two commits -- `79cd12e` and `39748e3` (the unmodified upstream import).  So the arm went from upstream's form straight to the fix, in `79cd12e`.\n  3. **The commit message agrees and adds the scope**: *'#502 m88kexcdeliver (detector 46 -> 59 rows, **no product change after the fix**)'* -- i.e. that commit carries the product change, and passes 9-12 refined the DETECTOR only.\n\n*** THE TITLE UNDERCOUNTS ITS OWN FIX: it names FIVE classes and SEVEN are repaired *** (BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION and ERROR are also tagged).  Left as-is rather than rewritten, because the title is what the row was FILED as and the extra two are a fact about the repair; noted here so a future reader does not read the title as the repair's denominator.\n\n*** STATE DELIBERATELY LEFT AT `in work`, AND THE RESTRAINT IS THE POINT. ***  Adding the commit is unambiguous -- the fix is in the tree and three checks agree.  **Closing it is not**: the sibling row [[m88kexcdetectorl2]] concerns an L2 in this very detector, and the most recent records commit (`8d372aa`) reports that *a HIGH row's 'measured clean sweep' was neither a sweep nor a failure -- the mutants target exception vectors the probe never drives*.  **A row covering work where part shipped and part is disputed is still live**, and this project's own rule is that a state misdescribing substance is the defect, in either direction.\n\n**PROVENANCE: found by a sweep for live rows with an EMPTY `commits` whose id appears in COMMITTED `src/` -- 23 of 284 such rows matched.**  *That match is a CORRELATE, not a finding: a tag in source can equally be a comment NAMING the row as still-open (`#497` owed exactly such a comment for [[sgilinearoob]]).  So the other 22 were sent for classification rather than corrected in bulk* -- which is this project's own deriver-keys-on-a-correlate rule, applied before the error rather than after it.\n\n**This is the SECOND ledger-is-stale-direction finding today**, after [[diskimagesign]].  Both run opposite to the twelve bug-file findings in the same sweep, and `check_bugfile_sync.py` checks only ledger -> bug file, so **neither direction of this pair is covered by anything** -- see [[bugfilereverse]].",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38445:   "id": "m88kexcdetectorl2",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38450:   "witness": "harness:regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/{BASE,M1,M2}.log -- MEASURED clean sweep, agy 2026-09-07; three distinct binaries verified, all fresh",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38451:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the RETROSPECTIVE pass 2 on `79cd12e' -- the receipt that round never filed, which\n`precommit_check.sh' section F refused the next commit over.  **This is the pass's one actionable finding.**\n\n**THE PREDICTION, from the grok seat, verbatim:** *\"The `#502` detector, asked of the shipped composite, has\none clause-(d) mutant I predict sweeps **59/59**: `implemented = true` -> `implemented =\n(cpu->machine->ncpus < 2)' on the seven arms.  I could not build it (read-only seat; gates forbidden).  If\nthat score is real it is **L2 of the detector**, not of `cpu_m88k.c`.\"*\n\n**IT IS THE CANONICAL CLAUSE-(d) SHAPE**: the guard is kept and its repair is CONDITIONED on a field the\nguarded body reads -- `if (X)` -> `if (X && Y)` on the dominating predicate.  The floor says a clause-(d)\nmutant that sweeps a detector clean is an **L2 breach, repaired in-round however late it is found**.\n\n*** STATUS: PREDICTED, NOT MEASURED.  That distinction is the whole disposition. ***  The floor's L2 rule\nfires on a MEASURED false pass; a prediction is a candidate.  grok said plainly it could not build the mutant\n-- read-only seat, gates forbidden -- and did NOT dress the prediction as a score.  Recording it as owed rather\nthan acting on it is the honest reading, and recording it at all is what stops it becoming an unread review.\n\n**WHY IT IS CREDIBLE ENOUGH TO MEASURE:** the same seat, in the same answer, correctly settled two other\npremises by re-derivation -- that `machine->ncpus` has three assignment sites all preceding any device (so\n`#503`'s DEVINIT snapshot is stable, which is what minimax had named as the settling fact and could not check\nfrom the packet), and that `#502` delivers all seven classes at BOTH sites, i.e. it is NOT the `#499`\none-cell-repair shape that glm's arithmetic had suggested.  A seat that got two checkable things right in the\nsame answer has earned the build.\n\n**TO MEASURE IT:** the mutant is one edit repeated on seven arms, scored against the shipped\n`regress/m88k_exc_probe.py` (59 rows).  This is what the **agy measurement reserve exists for** -- it is the\nonly compile-and-measure seat not behind the Agent-tool transport, and Opus 5 / Fable 5 are held for the\n2026-09-09 adjudication.  agy has no sandbox, so the brief must say: COPY the tree, build the COPY, never\nwrite to GXEMUL-SEC, `rm src/cpus/*.o' first, and check `[ binary -nt source ]' because MAKE_RC has been\nobserved reporting 0 on a failed build.\n\n**IF IT SWEEPS 59/59 it is L2 of the DETECTOR** -- `m88k_exc_probe.py`, not the product fix, which every seat\nagrees is in the right place.  **If it does not sweep, the row closes as a measured non-finding** with the\nscore recorded, which is a result and not a waste.\n\n*** SECOND, INDEPENDENT PREDICTION OF THE SAME HOLE -- FROM A DIFFERENT SEAT, ON A DIFFERENT MUTANT.\nThat is the one kind of agreement this project counts as evidence. ***\n\nThe `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute (the CLI being 403-walled) returned **NOT A CLEAN RATIFY** and predicted:\n\n    *\"a one-line deletion of `implemented = true;' on an unprobed exception class re-opens #502 exactly,\n     and every probe row named in this packet stays green.\"*\n\n**It is a DIFFERENT mutant from grok's** -- a bare DELETION on a class the probe never drives, where grok's is\na CONDITIONED repair (`implemented = (cpu->machine->ncpus < 2)`) on the seven arms.  Two seats, two shapes, one\nconclusion: **the 59-row detector does not cover the `implemented' flag across the exception classes.**\n\n**AND KIMI'S IS THE CHEAPER MUTANT TO BUILD AND THE HARDER ONE TO DISMISS**: a deletion needs no reachability\nargument at all, and its premise -- that some exception class is UNPROBED -- is checkable by reading the probe's\nrow list against the switch's case labels, with no build required.  **That check should run BEFORE any mutant\nis compiled**: if every class is probed, kimi's prediction dies on the spot for free; if one is not, the mutant\nis written against that class and the measurement is aimed rather than speculative.\n\n*Note the transport, per the doctrine: the substitute is PACKET-FED, so this is attack REASONING, not a repo\nfact.  It cannot know which classes the probe drives -- which is exactly why it named the check rather than\nasserting the answer.*\n\n**TWO FURTHER CANDIDATES from the same seat, recorded here so they are not lost, and NOT actioned:**\n* *\"this commit makes an admittedly manual-inconsistent delay-slot path reachable by ordinary faults and the\n  probe pins the wrong values green -- file it at minimum, L2/L3-candidate if the detector contract claims\n  shadow fidelity.\"*  Whether the contract makes that claim is a reading of the probe header, not of this\n  packet.\n* the `#503` comment's *\"two exits\"* defence has a **program-order hole** -- connects run before the\n  machine-layer exit -- which its own `-n 5` measurement cannot exclude.  **grok settled the ADJACENT premise**\n  (no post-DEVINIT write to `machine->ncpus`) but not this one, which is about the ORDER of two init-time\n  exits rather than about mutation.  The two seats are not in conflict; they checked different things.\n\n*** THE FREE CHECK RAN, AND IT CONFIRMS THE PREMISE.  RAISED MED -> HIGH.  Measured 2026-09-07, no build\nrequired -- exactly as predicted, the check settled it for nothing. ***\n\nI said kimi's prediction was the cheaper one because its premise -- *some exception class is UNPROBED* -- is\ncheckable by reading the probe's rows against the switch's labels.  Run:\n\n    implemented = true sites          11   (NOT the 7 the round's own narrative implies)\n      on RESET                        line 700   <-- probe drives no such row\n      on INTERRUPT                    line 705   <-- probe drives no such row\n      on INSTRUCTION_ACCESS           line 710   <-- probe drives no such row\n      on DATA_ACCESS                  line 715   <-- probe drives no such row\n      on the seven fixed arms         lines 720-750, ALL probed\n\n**SO THE MUTANT EXISTS AND ITS TARGET IS NAMED: delete `implemented = true;' at line 700, 705, 710 or 715.**\nThe probe's 59 rows drive bounds / div0 / error / misalign / ovf / priv / unimpl / sfu1 and the trap family;\n**none of them drives RESET, INTERRUPT, INSTRUCTION_ACCESS or DATA_ACCESS**, so a deletion there is invisible\nto every row -- which is the clean sweep, reached by the smallest possible edit.\n\n**AND TWO OF THE FOUR ARE ORDINARY GUEST TRAFFIC.**  `DATA_ACCESS' and `INSTRUCTION_ACCESS' are page and bus\nfaults -- the commonest exceptions a real guest takes.  A regression that silently re-opened `#502` for those\nwould stop EVERY guest CPU on an ordinary fault, which is precisely the defect `#502` spent twelve passes\nclosing.  `RESET' and `INTERRUPT' are less obviously guest-driven and their reachability is NOT established\nhere.\n\n*** THE PACKET-FED SEAT REASONED TO A CORRECT STRUCTURAL CONCLUSION IT COULD NOT VERIFY, AND SAID SO. ***  It\ncannot read the probe or the switch; it inferred from the packet that a flag set per-class and a detector\ndriving some classes must leave others uncovered, and it named the check instead of asserting the answer.\nThat is the transport preamble working, and it is worth more than an unconditioned agreement from a seat that\ncould have checked and did not.\n\n**WHAT REMAINS UNMEASURED, and it is the only thing left: THE SCORE.**  The premise is verified by reading;\nwhether the mutant actually scores 59/59 needs a build.  Do not record a breach until it is measured -- the\nfloor's L2 rule fires on a MEASURED false pass, and this row exists partly to keep that line sharp.\n\n**grok's mutant is now the SECONDARY candidate**: it needs the conditioned form to be reachable, while this\none needs only a deletion on a line that is already there.  Measure this one first.\n\n*** GRADING CORRECTED BY ME, WITHIN THE HOUR, BEFORE ANY REPAIR WAS ATTEMPTED.  I recorded this as a\nCONFIRMED L2 FLOOR BREACH.  THE MEASUREMENT STANDS; THE GRADING DOES NOT, AND I SHOULD NOT HAVE ASSERTED\nIT. ***\n\n**WHAT I MISSED: the four uncovered arms are NOT part of `#502`'s fix.**  The probe's own docstring says so\nin its second paragraph:\n\n    `cpu_m88k.c`'s exception switch marks only RESET / INTERRUPT / INSTRUCTION_ACCESS / DATA_ACCESS\n    `implemented`; every other vector below M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START falls to a fallback ...\n\n**That is a description of the PRE-FIX state.**  Those four flags are pre-existing upstream code.  `#502`\nADDED SEVEN (`git show` on the commit: exactly 7 `implemented = true` lines added), and the probe DRIVES all\nseven.  So the detector covers the arms its round actually repaired.\n\n**THE TWO READINGS, and I am not entitled to pick between them unilaterally:**\n\n* **L2 FLOOR BREACH.**  Clause (d)'s DEPENDS-ON closure is *the predicates dominating the diff's edited\n  lines, walked to the ENCLOSING FUNCTION BOUNDARY*.  The `switch (vector)` dominates all eleven arms and\n  they share one function; `implemented` is the fix's own variable.  On that reading the four are inside the\n  closure and a clean sweep is a breach.\n* **CLAUSE-(e) COVERAGE DEBT.**  The four sites are not edited lines, existed before the round, and the fix's\n  repair is not nulled by deleting a flag it never set.  On that reading the detector defends what its round\n  changed, and the gap is debt on untouched code -- FILED, ranked, not repaired in a follow-up.\n\n**WHAT IS SETTLED EITHER WAY, and it is the part that matters: THE COVERAGE GAP IS REAL AND MEASURED.**  A\none-line deletion at four sites in a shipped file is invisible to a 59-row detector, and two of those sites\n(`DATA_ACCESS`, `INSTRUCTION_ACCESS`) are ordinary page and bus faults.  Nothing about the grading dispute\nchanges that number or that reachability.\n\n**WHY I RECORDED IT WRONG:** the premise I verified was *four `implemented = true` sites are unprobed*, which\nis true.  I then carried it straight to *therefore a clause-(d) breach* without checking WHICH SITES THE FIX\nTOUCHED -- the one fact that decides the closure.  It took one `git show` to settle and I ran it only after\nwriting the grading down.  **That is the premise-audit failure this project's own brief opener exists to\nprevent, committed by the person who writes the openers.**\n\n*Raised as a rule question rather than resolved: it turns on the DEPENDS-ON closure's boundary for a fix that\nadds instances of an EXISTING pattern, which is a class this project will meet again -- every de-fatalisation\nround that extends a flag or a guard already present has the same shape.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07 BY agy.  THE PREDICTION IS CONFIRMED: A CLEAN SWEEP, 59/59 -> 59/59, ZERO\nROWS REDDENED.  THIS IS NOW AN L2 FLOOR BREACH OF THE DETECTOR, NOT A PREDICTION. ***\n\n    BASELINE   M88KEXC_RESULT=59/59\n    MUTANT M1  M88KEXC_RESULT=59/59        rows reddened: NONE\n\n    freshness  [ /tmp/gxm88k/gxemul -nt .../cpu_m88k.c ]  passed on BOTH builds\n    sandbox    all work in /tmp/gxm88k; GXEMUL-SEC verified untouched by git status\n\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/agy_measure_20260907.txt`.  Brief: `MEASURE_brief.md`\nin the same directory.\n\n**THE MUTANT IS THE CANONICAL CLAUSE-(d) SHAPE** -- the guard is kept and its repair is conditioned\non a field the guarded body reads: `implemented = true;` -> `implemented = (cpu->machine->ncpus < 2);`.\nThe floor says a clause-(d) mutant that sweeps a detector clean is an **L2 breach, repaired in-round\nhowever late it is found**.\n\n*** THE SEVEN-VERSUS-ELEVEN DISCREPANCY IS RESOLVED, AND IT WAS PUT TO THE SEAT AS A QUESTION\nRATHER THAN CORRECTED SILENTLY. ***  This row said the edit applies to SEVEN arms; my grep found\nELEVEN.  agy derived the list and changed **all 11**, all inside the `switch (vector)` in\n`m88k_exception()`:\n\n    RESET, INTERRUPT, INSTRUCTION_ACCESS, DATA_ACCESS          (4, unannotated)\n    MISALIGNED_ACCESS, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE, PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION,\n    BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE,\n    INTEGER_OVERFLOW, ERROR                                    (7, annotated m88kexcdeliver)\n\n**The row's \"seven\" counted only the arms carrying the `/* m88kexcdeliver */` annotation** -- i.e.\nthe ones a previous round touched -- and silently excluded four pre-existing vectors in the same\ndispatch.  That is the count-deriver rule again: a number keyed on an ANNOTATION rather than on what\ndefines the class.  *The sweep is a sweep either way; the enumeration was wrong and is corrected.*\n\n**TWO INDEPENDENT READING-SEAT PREDICTIONS WERE RIGHT.**  grok named the mutant and the score from\nthe packet alone and said plainly it could not build it (read-only seat, gates forbidden); a second\nseat reached the same hole separately.  **The reading seats bought the CLASS and the measurement\nbought the INSTANCE** -- which is exactly the division of labour the carrier records, and it is the\nargument for handing a reading seat's named family straight to the measuring seat.\n\n*** WHAT IS OWED: THE REPAIR IS A DETECTOR ROW, NOT A PRODUCT CHANGE. ***  Every seat agrees the\nproduct fix is correctly placed; what is broken is `regress/m88k_exc_probe.py`'s coverage of it.  A\nrepair row must FAIL under this mutant, which means it has to exercise the dispatch with\n`machine->ncpus >= 2` and assert the exception is still delivered -- the detector's 59 rows evidently\nall run at the default one CPU, so the mutant's condition is true throughout and nothing observes it.\n\n**NOT BUILT IN THIS TURN**, and the reason is recorded rather than glossed: adding a row to a\n59-row detector perturbs the scores of every mutant already measured against it, so it owes its own\nmeasured table rather than being appended to a round that is closing.  Filed as the next action on\nthis row, with the mutant preserved and reproducible from the brief.\n\n*** THE CAUSE OF THE SWEEP IS CONFIRMED BY READING, AND IT PINS THE REPAIR EXACTLY. ***\n\n`m88k_exc_probe.py` **never passes `-n`.**  Every session launches:\n\n    os.execvp(BIN, [BIN, \"-V\", \"-E\", \"testm88k\", \"-M\", \"64\", \"0x10000:%s\" % stub])\n\nso all 59 rows run at the DEFAULT ONE CPU.  The mutant conditions the repair on\n`cpu->machine->ncpus < 2`, which is therefore **true in every row**, `implemented` keeps the value\nit had before the mutation, and nothing observes the change.  **That is the whole explanation for\n59/59 -> 59/59, and it is a property of the detector's launch line rather than of its oracles.**\n\n*The sibling probe already has the idiom:* `luna_absentcpu_probe.py:180` builds\n`[BIN, \"-V\"] + ([\"-n\", str(ncpus)] if ncpus > 1 else []) + [...]`, and that round used it to run a\nfour-CPU session (E: `-n 4`, four cpus / cpu3 deliver / others idle).\n\n> **THE REPAIR, precisely specified: one session launched with `-n 2` (or more) that drives an\n> exception and asserts it is still DELIVERED.**  Under the mutant, `ncpus < 2` is FALSE, so\n> `implemented` becomes false and the exception is not delivered -- the row reds.  On the shipped\n> source it is delivered and the row is green.  That is a clause-(d) kill by construction rather\n> than by luck.\n\n**ONE OPEN QUESTION, and it is the only thing between this design and a build: does `-E testm88k`\naccept `-n 2` at all, and does the second CPU change anything the existing oracles read?**  The luna\nround ran multi-CPU on a MACHINE (`-e luna-88k`) with a disk image and a boot; this probe uses a\nbare `-E testm88k` emulation with a stub.  **NOT CHECKED HERE** -- it needs one run, and a run is a\nprobe, which must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.\n\n**IF `-n` IS REJECTED OR INERT ON `testm88k`**, the repair needs a different observable for the same\npredicate -- e.g. a row on a machine that does supply multiple CPUs -- and that is a bigger change\nthan adding a session.  **Recording the fork now so the next session does not rediscover it.**\n\n**COST NOTE, unchanged and load-bearing:** adding any row to a 59-row detector perturbs the scores of\nevery mutant already measured against it, so this repair owes its own measured table (baseline plus\nthe twelve-odd mutants already on record) rather than being appended to a closing round.  That is why\nit is designed here and not built here.\n\n*** THE OPEN QUESTION IS CLOSED BY READING -- NO RUN NEEDED -- AND THE REPAIR IS DE-RISKED. ***\n\nI flagged one thing between the design and a build: does `-E testm88k` accept `-n 2`, and does a\nsecond CPU perturb the existing oracles?  Three reads settle it.\n\n**1.  `-n` IS ACCEPTED AND VALIDATED.**  `src/core/main.c:423-429`:\n\n        case 'n':\n                /*  atoi() accepted negative counts, which later\n                    feed malloc()/memset() size calculations.  */\n                m->ncpus = (int) parse_int_option('n', optarg, 1, 256);\n                machine_specific_options_used = true;\n\nRange 1..256, so `-n 2` is legal.  (Note the fork has already hardened this site against a negative\ncount reaching `malloc`.)\n\n**2.  THE CPUs ARE CREATED IN THE GENERIC PATH, NOT A MACHINE-SPECIFIC ONE.**  `src/core/emul.c:489`\nonwards allocates `m->cpus[m->ncpus]` and loops creating each one, with no machine-type gate.  So a\nbare `-E testm88k` emulation goes through exactly the same code and really does get two CPUs.\n\n**3.  THE BOOTSTRAP CPU IS DETERMINISTIC HERE, WHICH WAS THE REAL HAZARD.**  `emul.c:510-513` is\n\n        if (m->use_random_bootstrap_cpu)\n                m->bootstrap_cpu = random() % m->ncpus;\n        else\n                m->bootstrap_cpu = 0;\n\n**`random() % m->ncpus` would have made any multi-CPU row non-deterministic** -- a flaky detector\nrow is worse than a missing one.  It is guarded, and `use_random_bootstrap_cpu` is set in exactly\ntwo places, `emul_parse.c:490` (config-file) and `main.c:450` (an explicit CLI flag).\n**`machine_test.c` never sets it**, so `testm88k` bootstraps cpu0 deterministically.\n\n> **So the repair is buildable as designed: ONE session launched `-n 2`, driving an exception and\n> asserting it is still DELIVERED.  Under the mutant `ncpus < 2` is FALSE, `implemented` becomes\n> false, the exception is not delivered, and the row reds.  Deterministic on both sides.**\n\n*Recorded because the hazard was real and invisible from the design: I would have written the row,\nmeasured it green twice and red once, and spent the next session hunting a flake that was\n`random() % 2` in the emulator's own startup.  Three greps, no runs.*\n\n**WHAT REMAINS BEFORE IT SHIPS is only the cost already stated: adding a row to a 59-row detector\nperturbs every mutant score already measured against it, so this owes its own table -- baseline plus\nthe mutants on record -- rather than being appended to a closing round.**",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38457:     "note": "*** MEASURED.  BOTH MUTANTS SWEEP.  THE PREDICTION IS CONFIRMED AND THIS IS NOW AN L2 FLOOR BREACH. ***\n\n    BASE  59/59   M88KEXC_SELFTEST=OK, controls green (live=DONE, attrib=DONE)\n    M1    59/59   `implemented = true' deleted at DATA_ACCESS -- ZERO row diffs vs BASE\n    M2    59/59   `implemented = true' deleted at RESET       -- ZERO row diffs vs BASE\n\nLogs preserved at `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/{BASE,M1,M2}.log', each ending in `M88KEXC_DONE'.\n\n*** THE RESULT LOOKED LIKE AN APPARATUS FAILURE AND I CHECKED BEFORE RECORDING IT. ***  All three logs have\nthe SAME md5 (`898eb0a7...').  That is exactly what a true clean sweep looks like -- the mutant changes\nnothing the probe observes -- and it is ALSO exactly what measuring the SAME BINARY three times looks like.\nThe two are indistinguishable from the logs alone, so the logs alone cannot settle it.\n\n**The discriminator is whether the three builds actually differ, and they do:**\n\n    binaries   base 44feb40b...  m1 4bbd8368...  m2 f94f9156...   THREE DISTINCT, built 02:44/02:46/02:47\n    sources    base 3593a8a5...  m1 489fc1dd...  m2 bd5c9284...   THREE DISTINCT\n    sites      base 11           m1 10          m2 10             the mutant removed EXACTLY ONE\n    freshness  base FRESH        m1 FRESH       m2 FRESH          binary newer than its source\n\nSo the identical log md5 IS THE FINDING, not an artifact: the detector's output is byte-identical whether or\nnot those two arms set the flag.\n\n**THE SEAT DID THE JOB PROPERLY AND THAT IS WORTH RECORDING**, because this project's measurement seat runs\nwithout a sandbox: it copied the tree to its own `/tmp' clone before touching anything, built each mutant\nfrom a FRESH copy of the baseline rather than stacking them, ran `rm src/cpus/*.o' per build, verified\n`[ binary -nt source ]', showed the `diff' proving each mutant text LANDED, derived row diffs against the\nBASE log rather than echoing a summary, and copied every log to the durable path.  Nothing was written to\nany project tree.\n\n**DISPOSITION -- L2, but in a FOLLOW-UP COMMIT, not in-round.**  The floor says a clause-(d) clean sweep is\nrepaired in-round however late; the LATE-DISSENT rule governs here because this is a retrospective pass on a\nCLOSED round, and it routes a MEASURED FALSE PASS to L2/L3 **in a follow-up commit**.  `79cd12e' is not\nreopened and nothing is amended under its number.\n\n*** THE REPAIR IS NOT OBVIOUSLY `ADD FOUR ROWS', AND THE ROUND THAT TAKES THIS MUST NOT ASSUME IT IS. ***\nThe four uncovered classes are RESET, INTERRUPT, INSTRUCTION_ACCESS and DATA_ACCESS.  A row must DRIVE the\nexception it pins -- and it is not established that a probe can drive a RESET or an INTERRUPT from a guest\ninstruction stream at all.  DATA_ACCESS and INSTRUCTION_ACCESS are page and bus faults and look drivable;\nthe other two may not be.  **If a class cannot be driven, the honest repair is a CONTRACT disclosure naming\nit as uncovered -- clause (e) -- not a row that pretends to cover it.**  That is a design question for the\nrepair round and it is the reason this entry stops at the measurement."
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38467:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/agy_measure_20260907.txt",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38478:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/",
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38494:   "note": "*** THIS ROW CARRIED A TITLE AND AN EMPTY NOTE UNTIL 2026-09-10, WHILE HOLDING A MEASURED CLEAN SWEEP AND A RULE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT. ***  The evidence existed on disk the whole time in `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/'; nothing pointed at it.  This note is written from those artifacts, re-verified before recording -- it is a bare-title instance of the [[baretitle]] class, and the worst kind, because the row is HIGH and a seat held for it could not have known what it was being asked.\n\n*** THE QUESTION THIS ROW IS HELD FOR, stated so a seat does not have to reconstruct it: ***\n\n    A shipped detector scores 59/59 under mutants that null the repair of the guard it was\n    written to defend.  Floor clause (d) says a detector must fail when the fix is PRESENT and\n    its repair is NULLED, and calls a breach L2 -- replace in-round however late.  Clause (e)\n    says an escape above the floor is COVERAGE DEBT and is filed.\n\n    IS THIS AN L2 FLOOR BREACH OR CLAUSE-(e) DEBT?  The discriminator that matters: does\n    `implemented' fall inside the DEPENDS-ON enumeration of the round that shipped this\n    detector -- i.e. is it a predicate DOMINATING the diff's edited lines within the enclosing\n    function, or a body-read lvalue the guard could be re-aimed at -- or is it outside, in which\n    case the escape is real and the disposition is still FILE?\n\nTWO INDEPENDENT MUTANT FAMILIES, BOTH CLEAN SWEEPS, MEASURED ON DIFFERENT DAYS BY DIFFERENT APPARATUS.\n\n**(1) The 02:49 table, three preserved logs.**  `BASE.log', `M1.log' and `M2.log' are **BYTE-IDENTICAL** -- md5 `898eb0a70987b47795f4a61be1155441' for all three, 8,994 bytes each, each ending `M88KEXC_DONE' with `M88KEXC_RESULT=59/59'.  **Not one row's TEXT differs**, so the sweep is not merely score-equal: every per-row `RESULT=', pc, sxip, snip, sfip, psr, epsr and r2 value is the same under the mutants as under the baseline.  *The identities of M1 and M2 come from this row's TITLE (deletion at DATA_ACCESS and at RESET) and NOT from a preserved driver -- the driver was not kept, which is [[killdriverunTracked]] biting this row specifically.*\n\n**(2) The agy re-measurement, 21:22 the same day** (`agy_measure_20260907.txt', built and run in `/tmp/gxm88k', both freshness checks passed, `GXEMUL-SEC' verified unmodified).  A DIFFERENT mutant -- every `implemented = true;' in the exception dispatch replaced by `implemented = (cpu->machine->ncpus < 2);' -- and the report is `M88KEXC_RESULT=59/59' with reddened rows: **none**.\n\n*** AND IT CORRECTED THE CENSUS IN MY OWN BRIEF: ELEVEN SITES, NOT SEVEN. ***  The brief asked the seat to derive the list rather than trust my count, and flagged that I believed my own row was wrong.  It is: seven arms carry the `/* m88kexcdeliver */' annotation, and **four more un-annotated arms in the SAME `switch (vector)' inside `m88k_exception()' also assign `implemented'** -- RESET, INTERRUPT, INSTRUCTION_ACCESS and DATA_ACCESS.  All eleven were changed.  *The annotation marked the arms one ROUND touched, and a census keyed to it counts the round's footprint rather than the predicate's population -- the deriver-must-key-on-what-defines-the-class rule, in a fresh instance.*\n\n**WHY THE SWEEP IS PLAUSIBLE RATHER THAN SUSPICIOUS**, which the adjudication needs in order to rule: the probe drives a single-CPU machine, so `ncpus < 2' is TRUE at every site and the agy mutant is EQUIVALENT on that configuration -- a non-dying mutant is a RESULT, and this one's explanation is 'the target is unreachable in the rig', not 'the detector is blind'.  **That explanation does NOT cover the 02:49 pair**, which deletes the assignment outright; those two are the ones that need a disposition.\n\nOWED BEFORE OR ALONGSIDE THE RULING, and cheap: re-run the 02:49 pair with a PRESERVED driver so the mutant identities are attributable to an artifact rather than to a title, and record whether a multi-CPU configuration exists in which the agy mutant is non-equivalent.\n\nBOTH COUNTS RE-DERIVED IN THE LIVE TREE 2026-09-10 rather than transcribed from the seat's report, and the second attempt caught a trap in my own check:\n\n    grep -c 'implemented = true' src/cpus/cpu_m88k.c    ->  11   the population\n    grep -c 'm88kexcdeliver'     src/cpus/cpu_m88k.c    ->   9   NOT the annotated arms\n    grep -n 'm88kexcdeliver'     src/cpus/cpu_m88k.c    ->   7 annotated assignments (720-750)\n                                                            + 2 PROSE mentions (783, 930)\n\n**The bare `-c' says 9 and the annotated-arm count is 7**, because two occurrences are ordinary comment text -- one of which is the very sentence recording that *the seven trap-like classes above* were the round's subject.  A count keyed to a marker string counts every MENTION of the marker, including the prose that explains it; `-n' and a look is what separates them.  *So the seat's 7 is right, my first re-derivation would have contradicted it wrongly, and the way I found out was opening the lines rather than trusting either number.*\n\n*** THE RULE QUESTION IS MALFORMED AND THE 'MEASURED CLEAN SWEEP' IS PROBABLY NOT A MEASUREMENT AT ALL.  2026-09-11 (m88kexcdetectorl2/codex_rulequestion_20260911.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max), and the load-bearing claim was then VERIFIED HERE by opening the file. ***\n\nI asked whether the sweep is an L2 floor breach or clause-(e) debt.  **The answer refuses the forced choice, on a prior question I had not asked: do the mutants represent execution of the described deletions at all?  The source PREDICTS RED and the logs are byte-identical GREEN.**\n\n*** WHY DELETING `implemented = true;' AT A REACHED ARM CANNOT BE INVISIBLE.  `cpu_m88k.c:799-808`, read directly: ***\n\n        if (vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented) {\n                debugmsg_cpu(cpu, SUBSYS_EXCEPTION, \"unimplemented\", VERBOSITY_ERROR, ...);\n                for (int j = 0; j < cpu->machine->ncpus; ++j)\n                        cpu->machine->cpus[j]->running = false;     <-- STOPS THE CPU\n                m88k_pc_to_pointers(cpu);\n                return;                                             <-- DELIVERS NOTHING\n        }\n\nThat return precedes the SFRZ re-route, the EPSR save, the exception PSR update, the shadow-register updates, the vector-entry PC assignment, the delay-slot bookkeeping, the memory-transaction register writes and the vector-specific tail.  **A 59-row probe whose rows print pc, sxip, snip, sfip, psr, epsr and r2 for DELIVERED exceptions cannot be byte-identical across that.**  And single-CPU operation does not neutralise it -- the loop runs once and stops that CPU.\n\n**SO THE BYTE-IDENTICAL TRANSCRIPTS ARE EVIDENCE THE MUTATION DID NOT TAKE EFFECT, NOT EVIDENCE THE DETECTOR IS BLIND.**  I had read the md5 identity as unusually strong evidence of a sweep; it is unusually strong evidence of an APPARATUS FAILURE.  *This is the `t0_apparatus_failure` shape from `#522` exactly -- a table that looked healthy because the mutants were never applied -- and this row already carried the warning that M1 and M2 are identified only by this row's TITLE and not by any preserved driver ([[killdriverunTracked]]).*\n\n**AND THE CLAUSE-(d) CENSUS IS RIGHT FOR A REASON I HAD WRONG.**  The dominating guard is the COMPOUND condition above, and it does **not** dominate the earlier `implemented = true;` assignments -- it FOLLOWS them.  It **does** dominate the seven added TAIL cases at `:923`, and the probe header says the repair needs BOTH eligibility and a tail arm.  So an argument that looks only at the assignments and concludes *the check comes afterward, therefore outside (d)* misses the other fixed site.  `implemented` is also NOT an operand-swap candidate: its only read is in that guard, and assigning it in a switch arm is a write, not a body read.  **Eleven assignment sites are not eleven predicates**, and the annotation count does not set the floor.\n\n**A SECOND FLOOR ROUTE THE BRIEF MISSED:** if the seven annotated assignments were ADDITIONS in the adjudicated diff, deleting one also restores that site's previous missing-eligibility behaviour -- which makes clause **(b)** a direct floor argument, independent of (d).  Arguing about whether to call it *reinstatement* or *null repair* does not move it into (e).\n\n*** WHAT IS OWED NOW, AND IT IS NOT AN ADJUDICATION: RE-RUN FAMILY 1 WITH A PRESERVED DRIVER AND A MUTANT-TEXT-NOT-FOUND HARD FAIL, AND CONFIRM THE BINARY WAS REBUILT. ***  If the deletion is genuinely applied and the rows still do not move, that is a finding about the PROBE worth the flagship seat's time.  **Until then there is nothing coherent to adjudicate**, and sending this to a walled seat as a floor-versus-debt question would have spent its first answer on a contradiction.\n\n*The brief also failed to say WHICH assignments M1 and M2 delete -- the seven annotated arms and the four pre-existing ones need separate treatment -- which is the same defect in a different coat: a question built on an apparatus whose inputs were never recorded.*\n\n*** RESOLVED STATICALLY, 2026-09-11, AND IT IS A THIRD EXPLANATION THAT NEITHER I NOR THE REVIEW REACHED: THE MUTANTS TARGET ARMS THE PROBE NEVER DRIVES. ***\n\n**THE ROW'S OWN TITLE SAYS M1 AND M2 DELETE `implemented = true;` AT `DATA_ACCESS` OR `RESET` -- vectors 3 and 0.  THE PROBE DRIVES NEITHER.**  Measured, not argued:\n\n    grep -c 'V_RESET|V_DATA|V_INTERRUPT|V_INSTR'  regress/m88k_exc_probe.py   ->  0\n\nIts 62 rows drive `V_DIV0` (8), `V_OVF` (9), `V_MISALIGN` (4), `V_BOUNDS` (7), `V_ERROR` (10), `V_UNIMPL` (5), privilege (6), `V_SFU1` (114), the reserved vectors 11 / 50 / 127, and the user traps 128 / 450 / 496 / 511.  **Not one row drives vector 0, 1, 2 or 3** -- which are exactly the four UN-annotated arms, the ones outside the `m88kexcdeliver` round's footprint.\n\n*** SO THE BYTE-IDENTICAL TRANSCRIPTS ARE THE CORRECT AND EXPECTED RESULT.  The mutated code is never executed. ***\n\n**THREE PARTIES REASONED ABOUT THIS WITHOUT CHECKING WHICH VECTORS THE PROBE DRIVES, AND ALL THREE WERE WRONG:**\n  * **the original row** called it a floor breach -- it is not, because the detector never reaches the mutated arms;\n  * **I** read the md5 identity as evidence of an apparatus failure, on the strength of the `#522` `t0` precedent.  **Wrong**: the apparatus was fine and the mutation was simply inert;\n  * **the design review** said the source PREDICTS RED.  That is true *only for arms the probe drives*, and it was not given the probe's vector list -- it had the file and could have derived it, but my brief never raised the question, which is my defect and not its.\n\n*The review's structural findings STAND and are the lasting value of that pass:* the dominating guard does not dominate the earlier assignments but DOES dominate the seven added tail cases; `implemented` is not an operand-swap candidate; eleven assignment sites are not eleven predicates; and if the seven annotated assignments were additions, clause (b) is a direct floor argument independent of (d).  **Those remain true and would govern a mutant aimed at an arm the probe actually drives.**\n\n**DISPOSITION: NOT A FLOOR BREACH, NOT AN APPARATUS FAILURE, AND BARELY EVEN DEBT -- LOWERED TO LOW.**  A detector is not obliged to cover vectors outside its stated scope, and this probe's own header says in as many words that the switch marks RESET / INTERRUPT / INSTRUCTION_ACCESS / DATA_ACCESS separately from the trap-like classes it was built for.  **The honest residual is one clause-(e) sentence: the instrument does not cover vectors 0-3, and a mutation there is invisible to it by construction.**\n\n*** AND THE RULE QUESTION HELD FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT IS WITHDRAWN. ***  There is nothing to adjudicate: the choice between L2 and clause (e) presupposed that a mutant in the detector's reach had escaped, and none had.  **That is the outcome worth having from preparing a packet carefully -- the preparation dissolved the question instead of sharpening it**, and it cost one grep that could have been run when the row was filed.\n\n*If someone still wants the floor question answered for this detector, the mutant must be aimed at an arm the probe DRIVES -- `MISALIGNED_ACCESS`, `UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE`, `PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION`, `BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION`, `ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE`, `INTEGER_OVERFLOW` or `ERROR`, all seven of which are annotated -- and THAT is a measurement worth taking.*\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **\"Its 62 rows drive ...\" -- the probe has 61**, and the 62 is a marker-counting artefact of exactly the kind this row's own note describes elsewhere. ***\n\n`regress/m88k_exc_probe.py`'s `ROWS` list has **61** entries, and `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/BASE.log` has **61** `M88KEXC_ROW=` lines (51 DELIVER + 8 HALT + 2 DONE; 59 scored fault rows, matching `M88KEXC_RESULT=59/59`).\n\n**62 is exactly what `grep -c \"RESULT=\" BASE.log` returns, because the SUMMARY line `M88KEXC_RESULT=59/59` also matches its own pattern.**  *The vector enumeration in that sentence is otherwise exact -- it is only the count that was taken from a grep that counted itself.*"
HEAD:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:42807:   "state_note": "[2026-10-09] REVIEW HOLD DISCHARGED AND THE ROW CLOSED AGAIN; both new variants FILED as clause-(e) debt.  The four packet-fed seats the owner ordered re-fired answered (_scratchpad/panel_20261009_191953; packet regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/assertrow_review_20261009/ollama_packet_20261009.md): glm ROW OWED (`assert = 0;` -> `assert ^= 1;`), deepseek and kimi (kimi-k3:cloud) ROW OWED (`d->warned |=` -> `=`), minimax CLOSED.  The main loop MEASURED both variants on the 18-row HEAD probe before any ruling: base 18/18, X 18/18, W 18/18 (regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/assertrow_review_20261009/probe_*.raw; build_and_run.sh.txt), so minimax's 'no other one-token edit passes' is refuted by measurement.  PACKET DEFECT, recorded against the main loop: the packet inlined the 13-row 2026-09-08 instrument, not HEAD's 18-row detector, so these four answers settle the hold's narrow return-variant question only and earn NO credit as a review of the 18-row detector.  THE RULING TOOK THREE VERSIONS (regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/assertrow_review_20261009/RULING_PROVENANCE.txt): v1 was written by claude-opus-4-8 after two Fable refusals and is NOT credited; v2 (Fable 5.1) graded X FLOOR and W debt; GPT-6 Astra confirmed with five changes and DISSENTED on X (regress/artifacts/fablequeue8n_20261009/astra_confirm/codex_astra_n6pcc2.txt); v3 (Fable 5.1, regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/assertrow_review_20261009/fable5_ruling_v3_20261009.md) REVERSED v2 -- the C040 guarded block writes `assert` and tests nothing on it, and `if (assert)` after the block's closing brace cannot dominate the edited line, so by DEPENDS-ON's written bound X is clause (e).  DISPOSITIONS: X filed as pcc2c040assertxor (MED), W as pcc2c040warnedeq (MED), both with their 18/18 receipts; the return variant the hold asked about is caught (`c040 noassert`: 9/10, regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/killtable_20260908/M_return.raw; 14/16, ctlq_20260909/repaired_M_return.raw).  OWNER QUESTION, named by v3 and decided by neither seat: does a repair's own write target, tested by the enclosing function's next statement, enter DEPENDS-ON?  OWNER 2026-10-09: KEEP THE WRITTEN RULE -- both stay debt.  The stale dev_pcc2.c comment beside the C040 arm is corrected by #560's held comment batch (staged from HEAD).  ||  FILED 2026-09-08 as `#512`'s own declared floor breach.  **The round graded it a BREACH rather than clause-(e) debt on purpose**: `assert = 0` is the repair's effect, so nulling it is (d)'s canonical form and sits inside the closure.  THE SHAPE OF THE REPAIR: breakpoint + `continue` so host-timer callbacks run, T1 enabled with a small compare so a timer interrupt goes pending, MIEN and C040 both set, and the oracle is the `debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\")` at the foot of reassert_interrupts() reading `no assert`.  *That channel IS visible in a stepped capture -- what cannot vary is the STATE, because no guest store can set an ICR INT bit.*\n\n*** THE ORACLE THIS ROW WAS FILED WITH IS WRONG, AND THE codex SEAT HAD ALREADY SAID SO IN AN ANSWER\nI HAD RECORDED AS \"not yet fully mined\".  Corrected 2026-09-08, VERIFIED BY LINE ORDER. ***\n\nThe row proposed reading `debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\")` for `no assert`.  **That\nmessage EXECUTES BEFORE BOTH SUPPRESSIONS:**\n\n    :334   debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\" ...)   <-- prints `assert' HERE\n    :339   if (!(GENCTL & PCC2_MIEN))       assert = 0;\n    :364   if (GENCTL & PCC2_C040)        { ... assert = 0; }\n    :374   if (assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT ... else INTERRUPT_DEASSERT\n\nSo it reports the PRE-suppression value and is **green under exactly the mutant it would be chosen\nto catch**.  codex: *\"do not use the existing `IPL ... => ASSERT' debug message as the line-state\noracle: it executes before MIEN suppression and before the proposed C040 suppression.  Observe the\ninterrupt callback/state.\"*\n\n> **THE CORRECT ORACLE IS THE INTERRUPT CALLBACK/STATE, not the diagnostic.**  A free-running probe\n> is still required for the STATE (an ICR INT bit cannot be set by a guest store), but the CHANNEL\n> changes: observe delivery, or instrument the callback -- never the pre-suppression debug line.\n\n*This is the channel-line check failing on a row I wrote while quoting the channel-line check.  A\nrow whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous by construction -- and here\nthe channel was not merely invariant, it was measured at the wrong POINT IN THE FUNCTION.*\n\n*** THE \"NEEDS A FREE-RUNNING PROBE\" WARRANT IS WRONG.  Refuted at pass 2 by grok and VERIFIED here\n2026-09-08. ***\n\n`pcctwo_interrupt_common()` ORs `PCC2_IRQ_INT` onto SCSIICR/SCCTX/SCCRX when those lines assert, and\n`dev_clmpcc.c:88-89` calls `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq_scc_tx)` **synchronously inside a guest store to\nCLMPCC_REG_IER**; `machine_mvme88k.c` constructs clmpcc with its irq wired to pcc2.  **So the state\nis reachable while single-stepping** -- `st.b` IEN|IL to SCCTX, `st.b` to the clmpcc IER, `st.b`\n`C040|MIEN` (0x06) to GENCTL.\n\n**THIS ROW IS THEREFORE AN IN-ROUND L2 OBLIGATION, NOT A FILED ONE**, and `pcc2c040kill` has been\nreopened accordingly.  The remaining difficulty is the ORACLE, not the state: the `debug()` line is\nbefore both suppressions, so the observable must be the interrupt line itself -- `INTERRUPT_ASSERT`\n/ `cpu->cd.m88k.irq_asserted` / guest delivery.\n\n*Two wrong reasons in a row on this same question -- first \"the channel is invisible\" (false: it\nprints while stepping), then \"the state is unreachable\" (false: another device's store sets it).\nThe conclusion \"no row today\" was right both times and neither reason was.*\n\n*** THE VEHICLE EXISTS AND IS SHIPPED.  `regress/m88k_exc_probe.py` IS ALREADY AN m88k FREE-RUNNING\nEXCEPTION-DELIVERY PROBE.  Found 2026-09-08 by looking instead of reasoning. ***\n\nIts own header describes the shape this row needs, verbatim:\n\n> *\"seeded with `rN=`, a `br .` (c0000000) planted at every vector entry so a delivered exception\n> spins rather than executing zeros, `breakpoint add <8*vector>`, `continue`, then `reg` and\n> `reg 0,0`.\"*\n\n**So the repair is a COPY of a proven in-tree shape, not a new capability.**  That matters for the\ndisposition: \"needs a different probe shape\" sounded like a capability gap and is really a\nhalf-day's careful work against an existing template.\n\n**THE ORACLE, now that both halves are settled:**\n\n    STATE   (stepped-reachable, per grok/pass 2) st.b IEN|IL -> SCCTX; st.b -> CLMPCC_REG_IER,\n            whose handler calls INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq_scc_tx) synchronously inside the store;\n            pcctwo_interrupt_common() then ORs PCC2_IRQ_INT into SCCTX.\n    ORACLE  (needs FREE-RUNNING) the m88k INTERRUPT vector entry.  Plant `br .' there,\n            `breakpoint add', `continue'.  Breakpoint FIRES  = the line asserted = the mutant.\n            Loop survives to timeout                          = suppressed  = the fix.\n\n> **WHY THE ORACLE STILL NEEDS FREE-RUNNING EVEN THOUGH THE STATE DOES NOT** -- and this refines\n> grok's *\"the obstacle is the oracle, not the state\"* rather than contradicting it.  The only\n> difference between the fix and the mutant is `INTERRUPT_ASSERT` versus `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` on\n> `d->cpu_irq`; `cpu->cd.m88k.irq_asserted` is a HOST-side field with no guest-readable reflection,\n> so the sole guest-visible consequence is DELIVERY -- and `cpu_dyntrans.c` suppresses interrupt\n> dispatch while single-stepping by design (*\"Do not cause interrupts while single-stepping\"*).\n> **State: stepped.  Delivery: free-running.  Both facts are needed and neither alone settles it.**\n\n*Note the adjacency to `m88kexcdetectorl2`, which is a HIGH row about that same probe.  These are\nseparate rows and must not be merged -- same vehicle, different defect, different oracle.*\n\n*** THE DESIGN REVIEW CAUGHT TWO DEFECTS BEFORE A LINE WAS WRITTEN, AND THE FIRST IS THAT THE ROW\nWOULD HAVE PASSED ITS OWN MUTANT.  codex, 2026-09-08, VERDICT: BUILD-WITH-REVISIONS. ***\n\n### 1.  *** THE PROPOSED DEFECT ROW STILL PASSES THE `return` MUTANT. ***\n\n**GENCTL resets to ZERO**, so MIEN is clear throughout the setup and every completed recalculation\nDEASSERTS the CPU line.  At the final GENCTL write:\n\n    shipped repair            deasserts the line          -> no interrupt   PASS\n    `assert = 0' DELETED      asserts the line            -> interrupt      caught\n    `assert = 0' -> `return'  leaves the line UNCHANGED   -> no interrupt   *** PASSES ***\n\n> *\"Deleting the assignment PERMITS ASSERTION; returning PRESERVES THE INCOMING LINE STATE.  The\n> callbacks simply assign `irq_asserted = 1` or `0`; returning invokes neither.\"*\n\n**My §3 treated the two mutants as interchangeable.  They are not**, and the row was built around the\none that happens to be easier to catch.\n\n**THE FIX IS A PRECONDITION, not a new oracle: write MIEN-ONLY GENCTL IN BOTH ROWS FIRST**, so the\nCPU line is already ASSERTED before the operation under test.  Then `return` leaves it asserted, the\ninterrupt is delivered, and the row reddens.\n\n### 2.  *** THE ORACLE WAS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL.  VERIFIED IN THE CODE. ***\n\n`session()`'s `rd()` tests `until in buf` **against the ENTIRE ACCUMULATED TRANSCRIPT**, at the top\nof its loop -- and `alive = rd(settle)` has already drained the boot banner, which ends at a\n`GXemul>` prompt.  So `(\"continue\", grace, \"GXemul>\")` returns **True with ZERO READS**.  codex\nexercised the extracted reader in memory and got exactly that.\n\n**And `how == \"no-breakpoint\"` is not the negative oracle I took it for**: it establishes a MISSING\nTOKEN, not successful execution without delivery.  The runner deliberately preserves that status\nthrough forced termination and unreaped outcomes, *\"so a C040-specific hang or unexpected guest halt\ncan satisfy a timeout-only negative oracle.\"*\n\n### 3.  THE REVISED DESIGN (to build)\n\n1. Fresh machine per row; perform setup while STEPPING.\n2. Establish the pending SCC TX request, then write **MIEN-only GENCTL in BOTH rows** -- this is the\n   precondition from §1.  *Do not rewrite SCCTX afterwards: its write arm CLEARS INT.*\n3. Final GENCTL write: MIEN-only for the control, MIEN|C040 for the defect row.\n4. `continue` into a FINITE guest path with a **separate normal-completion breakpoint**.  The\n   control must stop at the interrupt entry; the defect row must reach NORMAL COMPLETION without\n   hitting it.  **That gives the negative case affirmative evidence instead of a timeout.**\n5. Attribute stops by FRESH breakpoint evidence and PC.  **Treat timeout, unexpected stop or death\n   as FAILURE**, never as the negative result.\n\n### 4.  EVERY HARDWARE PREMISE CONFIRMED, one with a better warrant than I had\n\n* **PSR `0x80000000`** keeps supervisor mode, clears IND and SFRZ, preserves endianness; the\n  dispatch condition is `irq_asserted && !(PSR & IND)` outside single-stepping.  **It also checked\n  my `stcr` word against the decoder** -- it does select `stcr r5,cr1`.  *That is the\n  hand-assembled-encoding trap closed by someone other than its author.*\n* **VBR = 0 is right, and for a better reason than mine:** `mvmeprom.c:59` EXPLICITLY sets it to\n  zero, rather than it being an assumed reset value.\n* **Entry `0x8`**: interrupt is vector 1 and entry sets PC to `VBR + 8 * vector`.\n* **Source and priority:** SCCTX takes `0x13`; a qualifying IER write asserts SCC TX synchronously,\n  making it `0x33`; recalculation selects IPL 3; MASK starts at 0, so the comparison succeeds.\n\n### 5.  TWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN Q2 EXPECTATIONS\n\n* **\"Suppression by another route\" is NOT inherently a defect.**  *\"A behaviorally equivalent\n  implementation should pass.  The defect is violated behavior or collateral state changes, not\n  failure to use the local variable named `assert`.\"*\n* **My \"both rows fail to deliver\" escape is not a green pair** -- `(no interrupt, no interrupt)`\n  FAILS the positive control.  It becomes an escape only if the grader omits or neutralises that\n  control failure, which is an argument for asserting the control's outcome explicitly rather than\n  treating it as informational.\n\n**Residual classes it names as still uncovered by a delivery-only pair:** C040 suppression that\ncorrupts MASK/MIEN/pending state or IPL bookkeeping; suppression that stays stuck after C040 clears\n(needs a RECOVERY observation); and suppression that works only for the tested source, priority or\nfirst invocation.\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-08.  ALL THREE ESCAPING MUTANTS ARE KILLED. ***\n\n    BASE            11/11\n    M_d1_noassert    9/10   `assert = 0' DELETED        -- was 7/7 (a CLEAN SWEEP)   KILLED\n    M_return         9/10   `assert = 0' -> `return;'   -- the one-token pass-2 mutant KILLED\n    M_latchinside   10/11   `assert = 0' INSIDE the latch -- was 10/10               KILLED\n\n**The rows, and what each buys:**\n\n    irq pending     SCCTX reads 0x33 after the clmpcc IER store -- LIVENESS.  Without it, \"no\n                    interrupt\" is indistinguishable from \"no pending request was ever created\".\n    irq live        C040 CLEAR: the guest REACHES the interrupt vector (pc = 0x8).  POSITIVE\n                    CONTROL, and it is what makes the negative case meaningful.\n    c040 noassert   C040 SET on an ALREADY-ASSERTED line: reaches COMPLETION (pc = 0x1001c).\n    c040 noassert2  the SECOND C040 encounter, latch already set: also reaches completion.\n\n*** TWO DESIGN CORRECTIONS MADE THIS WORK, AND NEITHER WAS MINE. ***\n\n1. **The precondition.**  My first design wrote C040 into a RESET GENCTL, so MIEN was clear, the\n   line was already deasserted, and `return` -- which merely preserves the incoming line state --\n   would have PASSED.  Writing MIEN-only GENCTL FIRST, in BOTH rows, is what makes it catchable.\n   *codex, design review, before a line was written.*\n2. **The encounter axis.**  `assert = 0` moved inside the latch suppresses correctly on the FIRST\n   encounter and skips it on every later one, so a single-encounter row scores 10/10 against it.\n   **Closing the axis with one repeated write beats adding a row per mutant** -- the class-closure\n   rule rather than pass-per-instance.  *Predicted by codex on pass 1, measured here.*\n\n**AND THE NEGATIVE CASE IS AFFIRMATIVE, NOT A TIMEOUT.**  Both outcomes stop at a breakpoint and the\ndiscriminator is WHICH PC.  The obvious oracles were both unusable: the `debug()` line executes\nBEFORE the suppressions, and `(\"continue\", grace, \"GXemul>\")` returns True with ZERO READS because\n`rd()` matches against the whole accumulated buffer, which already holds a prompt.\n\n**RESIDUAL, disclosed rather than implied:** these four rows establish DELIVERY under one source, one\npriority and two encounters.  They do not cover C040 suppression that corrupts MASK/MIEN/pending\nstate, suppression that stays stuck after C040 CLEARS (a recovery observation), or other sources and\npriorities.  *And per codex: suppression by a different implementation route is NOT a defect -- \"the\ndefect is violated behavior or collateral state changes, not failure to use the local variable named\n`assert`.\"*\n\n*** COMMITTED AS `9fa07fe`, inside `#512`.  This row was FILED as coverage debt and CLOSED IN-ROUND\ninstead, which is what pass 2 required. ***  It never became a separate round: the four rows it\nspecified ship as part of the detector they were owed to.  *** SECTION O REPAIR 2026-09-17: the seven seat cells on this row carried ONE STAMPED NOTE from `panel_20260917_100431`, a SIX-ROW late review.  Each is now that seat's own answer, read from its own file. ***  **This was not tidying.  TWO REAL DEFECTS were sitting inside the stamp and neither had a cell of its own**: the `sh4_dmac_transfer` CHCR header L4 (grok, corrected in place) and `gdromdmaflood` (deepseek, filed MED and corroborated from source by codex).  *Section O's boilerplate cluster and section G's unread review are the same defect from two sides -- the stamp is what made the findings unfindable.*  **The seven answers were genuinely different**: seven distinct verdicts, from codex's DO NOT RATIFY to minimax's explicit refusal to overturn anything from the packet, and they disagreed about where the defects live -- glm called all four of its findings RECORDS defects, deepseek and codex found a product one.  A stamped note renders that as one voice.",

